Here’s a clear, step-by-step explanation of Disaster Recovery (DR) in AWS—focused on real-world implementation and interview readiness.
🔹 What is Disaster Recovery (Quick Recap)
Disaster Recovery ensures your application and data can recover quickly after failure (server crash, region outage, cyberattack).
🔹 Step-by-Step Disaster Recovery Implementation in AWS
✅ Step 1: Identify Critical Systems
👉 First, decide:
Which applications are business-critical
Which servers/databases must recover first
Example:
Web server (high priority)
Backup server (low priority)
✅ Step 2: Define RTO & RPO
👉 Decide recovery targets:
RTO → How fast system should recover
RPO → How much data loss is acceptable
Example:
RTO = 10 minutes
RPO = 5 minutes
✅ Step 3: Choose DR Strategy
Pick based on budget & requirement:
StrategyUse CaseBackup & RestoreLow cost, non-critical appsPilot LightModerate recoveryWarm StandbyFaster recoveryMulti-RegionCritical apps, zero downtime
✅ Step 4: Backup Your Data
Use:
Amazon S3
AWS Backup
👉 Actions:
Schedule automatic backups
Store backups in another region
✅ Step 5: Enable Data Replication
👉 Keep data synced between regions
Use:
Amazon RDS (Read Replica / Multi-AZ)
Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication
✅ Step 6: Setup Infrastructure in Secondary Region
👉 Create DR environment:
Launch EC2 instances (minimal or full setup)
Configure networking (VPC, subnets)
Use:
Amazon EC2
✅ Step 7: Configure Failover Mechanism
👉 Automatically switch traffic during failure
Use:
Amazon Route 53
Types:
Active-Passive
Active-Active
✅ Step 8: Setup Monitoring & Alerts
👉 Detect failures quickly
Use:
Amazon CloudWatch
Amazon SNS
✅ Step 9: Automate Recovery
👉 Reduce manual work
Use:
AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery
Actions:
Auto-launch servers
Auto-recover applications
✅ Step 10: Test Your DR Plan (Very Important)
👉 Regular testing ensures it works
Simulate server failure
Check failover time
Verify data integrity
